{"answerArea":{"calculator":true,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"Both $100$ and $9x^2$ are *perfect squares,* since $100=(\\blueD{10})^2$ and $9x^2=(\\greenD{3x})^2$.\n\n$100-9x^2 = (\\blueD{10})^2-(\\greenD{3x})^2$\n\nSo we can use the *difference of squares* pattern to factor.\n\n$\\blueD{a}^2 - \\greenD{b}^2 =(\\blueD{a}+\\greenD{b})(\\blueD{a}-\\greenD{b})$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"In this case, $\\blueD{a}=\\blueD{10}$ and $\\greenD{b}=\\greenD{3x}$:\n\n$(\\blueD{10})^2 - (\\greenD{3x})^2 =(\\blueD{10}+\\greenD{3x})(\\blueD{10}-\\greenD{3x})$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"In conclusion,\n\n$100-9x^2=(10+3x)(10-3x)$\n\n*Remember that you can always check your factorization by expanding it.*","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}}],"itemDataVersion":{"major":0,"minor":1},"question":{"content":"**Factor completely.**\n\n$100-9x^2=$ [[☃ expression 1]]","images":{},"widgets":{"expression 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"answerForms":[{"buttonSets":["basic"],"buttonsVisible":"focused","considered":"correct","form":true,"functions":["f","g","h"],"key":0,"simplify":false,"times":false,"value":"(10+3x)(10-3x)"},{"buttonSets":["basic"],"buttonsVisible":"focused","considered":"correct","form":true,"functions":["f","g","h"],"key":1,"simplify":false,"times":false,"value":"-(3x+10)(3x-10)"}],"buttonSets":["basic"],"functions":["f","g","h"],"static":false,"times":false},"static":false,"type":"expression","version":{"major":1,"minor":0}}}}}
